{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c4cb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a06f11-bf97-7731-9df0-9b191ed26b77"},"sequence":192,"recorded_at":1780531400000379,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"d35b2354-966f-42a4-bb95-82d384379b04","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"d35b2354-966f-42a4-bb95-82d384379b04","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"d35b2354-966f-42a4-bb95-82d384379b04"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"956f84a8-824d-4728-9c21-822c25e08480"},"task_id":"956f84a8-824d-4728-9c21-822c25e08480","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"956f84a8-824d-4728-9c21-822c25e08480","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.task-board/.resources/TASK-260830-1zvmw7/TASK-260830-1zvmw7_review-verdict-round2.md`.\n1|# TASK-260830-1zvmw7 — Review Verdict (round 2, CR rev 2)\n2|\n3|**Verdict: CHANGES REQUESTED.** Route to `to-dev`. One blocking finding (H1),\n4|two non-blocking (H2, H3).\n5|\n6|Scope reviewed: the uncommitted working tree at base `9481a20`. I recomputed the\n7|working-tree tree OID with a scratch index before and after every probe: both\n8|times `d99bf886589f38b06af25e8a381285d151a1817b`, byte-identical to the CR\n9|candidate. `manifest.go` sha256 re-verified as\n10|`97edf447a3c5a800321dd826bb55e9cc1a668af25472c9981ad6840199ea688b` after each of\n11|the four batteries; all ten package/traceability source files re-checked against\n12|a pre-review `shasum` baseline at the end.\n13|\n14|Baseline re-run by me, not accepted from the run: `go build ./...`,\n15|`go vet ./...`, `gofmt -l .` exit 0; `go test ./... -count=1` green across 14\n16|packages; package coverage 88.6%; `tracecheck -root .` exit 0 reporting\n17|`acceptance_cases=75`.\n18|\n19|---\n20|\n21|## Headline ratios\n22|\n23|| Measure | Round 1 | Round 2 |\n24|| --- | ---: | ---: |\n25|| Refusal assertions that check which arm fired | 0 / 29 | **every executed arm identity, 76 / 76** |\n26|| Executed arm **sites** whose identity is asserted | — | **85 / 85** |\n27|| Arm sites executed by the suite | 71 / 121 | **85 / 119** |\n28|| Mutants killed | 64 / 96 | **91 / 95** |\n29|| Resurrections among round-1 kills | — | **0** |\n30|| G-A gate deletions that fail by naming the right arm | 0 / 6 | **17 / 17** |\n31|| G-C aliasing positive control | survives | **killed** |\n32|\n33|---\n34|\n35|## G-A — the arm-shift premise, tested rather than accepted\n36|\n37|The rework's premise was that asserting `*Error.Detail` closes F2/F3/F5\n38|structurally. I re-ran the battery over the four §4.B gates, F3's\n39|untrusted-substitution gate at **both** its sites, F4's expiry boundary, and the\n40|F5 closed-schema class on `ParseProbe`, `ParseEvidence` and `ParseManifest` —\n41|17 gates, each grep-anchored (count == 1), `go vet`-checked, run, reverted.\n42|\n43|**All 17 are killed, and every one of them fails with `error = <nil>`.** Not one\n44|fixture slides onto a lower arm. That is the strongest outcome available: with\n45|the gate removed the tuple reaches admission, so the test fails because the gate\n46|admitted what it must reject, not because the refusal landed somewhere else.\n47|\n48|| Gate deleted | Test that fails | Failure |\n49|| --- | --- | --- |\n50|| `checkClaimRelation` omission loop | `TestReconcileRefusals/omitted_manifest_claim` | `error = <nil>` want `probe omission of manifest claim` |\n51|| probe static claim echo equality | `.../static_echo_drift` | `error = <nil>` want `probe static claim echo` |\n52|| `checkProbeMembership` protocol half | `.../protocol_not_member` | `error = <nil>` want `probe protocol membership` |\n53|| `checkEvidenceSet` `!claim.Value` half | `.../evidence_for_false_claim` | `error = <nil>` want `evidence claim binding` |\n54|| `checkManifestRecordBinding` digest | `TestAdmitProbeExternalRealm` | `error = <nil>` want `executable substitution` |\n55|| `checkProbeIdentity` digest | `TestReconcileRefusesExecutableSubstitution` | `error = <nil>` want `executable substitution` |\n56|| liveness upper bound `!Before`→`After` | `.../evidence_expires_at_admission_instant` | `error = <nil>` want `evidence liveness` |\n57|| `ParseProbe` schema / version / `ParseID` / kind / digest↔kind (5) | `TestProbeDocumentRefusals/*` | `error = <nil>` at the named arm |\n58|| `ParseEvidence` schema / version / `ParseID` / protocol major (4) | `TestEvidenceDocumentRefusals/*` | `error = <nil>` at the named arm |\n59|| `ParseManifest` kind | `TestManifestDocumentRefusals/unknown_implementation_kind` | `error = <nil>` want `manifest implementation kind` |\n60|\n61|F3 is worth calling out: the fixture is driven through `Registry.AdmitProbe`,\n62|the real production entry point, with the probe deliberately *tracking* the\n63|foreign digest so probe↔manifest identity holds and only the manifest↔record\n64|gate can refuse. That is the correct way to isolate two arms that share a code\n65|and a detail string, and the test comment says so.\n66|\n67|### The ratio that matters\n68|\n69|Method: enumerate every `mismatchf(...)`/`integrityFailure(...)`/`Detail: \"...\"`\n70|site in `manifest.go` by walking the file; map each to its narrowest containing\n71|block in a `-covermode=count` profile of the shipped suite; extract the asserted\n72|detail from every `requireRefusal`/`requirePinRefusal` call and table `detail:`\n73|field.\n74|\n75|* **119 arm sites**, 85 executed, 34 never executed.\n76|* **85 / 85 executed sites have their arm identity asserted** somewhere in the\n77|  suite. 76 / 76 executed distinct arm identities are named.\n78|* Round 1's figure was 0 / 29. The structural change is real.\n79|\n80|Stated bound on that ratio: 19 of the 85 executed sites share a detail string\n81|with another site (`document members`, `document member type`, `document\n82|syntax`, `document digest`, `document timestamp`, `document string bound`,\n83|`capability vocabulary`, `operation vocabulary`, `evidence realm binding`,\n84|`executable substitution`). For those the assertion names the **rule**, not the\n85|**site**. 66 executed sites carry a file-unique name and are pinned exactly.\n86|\n87|---\n88|\n89|## H1 (blocking) — a fifth `checkClaimRelation` arm still deletes cleanly, and it admits an undeclared capability\n90|\n91|`checkClaimRelation` has five arms. The rework gave four of them narrowed\n92|fixtures. The first one has none:\n93|\n94|```go\n95|case proved.Origin == OriginStatic && !exists:\n96|    return mismatchf(\"probe static claim without manifest\")\n97|```\n98|\n99|Neuter it (`_ = static`) and **the whole shipped suite stays green**. The arm is\n100|never executed by any test — coverage hit count 0.\n101|\n102|This is not a cosmetic gap. I built the bypass through the real entry points\n103|(`ParseManifest` → `ParseProbe` → `Reconcile`, pin fixtures, real signatures): a\n104|probe that declares `graceful_stop` with `origin: static` while the manifest\n105|declares only `headless_creation`.\n106|\n107|| Code | `Reconcile` result |\n108|| --- | --- |\n109|| HEAD | `terminal_backend_manifest_probe_mismatch at probe static claim without manifest`, `admitted=[]` |\n110|| gate neutered | `err=<nil>`, **`admitted=[graceful_stop headless_creation]`** |\n111|\n112|A backend self-asserts static origin for a capability its Manifest never\n113|declared, and the capability is admitted. The manifest→probe omission loop\n114|cannot catch it — it checks the other direction. This is the self-minted-claim\n115|shape, on the exact object this leaf exists to gate.\n116|\n117|The round-2 premise was that arm assertions close the class \"structurally rather\n118|than case by case\". Four of five arms in one `switch` got cases; the fifth did\n119|not. Round 1 already scored this function as a `sliver` (area 11, 1/3). The\n120|class is not closed.\n121|\n122|**Required:** one `TestReconcileRefusals` row whose probe carries a static-origin\n123|claim absent from the manifest, asserting `probe static claim without manifest`.\n124|\n125|## H2 (non-blocking) — `ParseEvidence`'s platform vocabulary is the F5 shape, unclosed\n126|\n127|`platform, err := scalar.ParsePlatform(platformName)` → `evidence platform`.\n128|Narrow to `platform, _ :=` and the suite stays green; the arm has 0 coverage\n129|hits. `ParseProbe` has `probe platform`, `ParseManifest` has `platforms\n130|vocabulary`, both tested. This is precisely the \"clause present in one context\n131|profile and absent from the other\" shape F5 named, in the one parser the rework\n132|did not extend for platform. A bad platform would probably still be refused\n133|downstream by `evidence tuple binding` or the signature, so the impact is\n134|defence-in-depth — but the gate's own class is unmeasured.\n135|\n136|## H3 (non-blocking) — `claim shape` unmeasured\n137|\n138|`parseClaim`'s `raw.(map[string]any)` guard narrows to `object, _ :=` with a\n139|green suite; 0 coverage hits. A non-object claim element then falls to\n140|`checkExactMembers` and refuses as `document members`, so it stays fail-closed —\n141|but the refusal is arm-blind, which is the property this round was about.\n142|\n143|---\n144|\n145|## G-B — resurrections: none\n146|\n147|Both round-1 batteries re-run in full, each mutant grep-anchored (count == 1),\n148|`go vet`-checked, run, reverted, with the file hash re-verified at the end.\n149|\n150|| Battery | Killed | Survived | Other |\n151|| --- | ---: | ---: | --- |\n152|| Battery 1 (60) | 46 | **0** | 12 COMPILE-FAIL (formulation artifacts: deleting the code leaves an unused binding), 2 ANCHOR-BAD |\n153|| Battery 2 (47) | 43 | 4 | 0 |\n154|| Re-anchored M37, M58 | 2 | 0 | anchors moved by the F1/F8 edits |\n155|| **Unique measured 95** | **91** | **4** | |\n156|\n157|The 12 battery-1 compile-fails are exactly the 12 whose compilable variants\n158|battery 2 carries, and all 12 are killed there. **Every mutant round 1 recorded\n159|as KILLED is still KILLED.** Nothing was loosened to close a finding.\n160|\n161|The 4 survivors are the four round 1 declared, unchanged:\n162|\n163|* `M71`/`M72` — JCS replaced by raw `json.Marshal`. **The rework did not change\n164|  this and I am not claiming it did.** The three closed schemas still carry only\n165|  ASCII keys and no numeric members, so Go's marshalling and RFC 8785 agree byte\n166|  for byte and no fixture can distinguish them. Reported as **unknown**, not as\n167|  covered.\n168|* `M92` (nil-registry guard in `AdmitProbe`) and `M95` (absent-vs-null in\n169|  `digestOrNullMember`) — near-equivalent; `Resolve` has its own nil guard and\n170|  `checkExactMembers` already guarantees presence. Counted as survivors for\n171|  honesty, per the round-1 convention.\n172|\n173|Round 1's protected areas all hold: `UnsignedEvidenceBytes` narrowing 20/20 this\n174|round (M16-M22, M61-M70, M15, M75 — every member drop, the domain separator and\n175|its NUL), `GenerationDigest` 4/4 including domain truncation, signature 2/2\n176|including forged key and nil verifier, tuple/coverage/ID-set 12/12.\n177|\n178|### Round-1 findings\n179|\n180|| Finding | State | Evidence |\n181|| --- | --- | --- |\n182|| F1 `CheckOperation` refused `manifest`/`probe` | **closed** | fix pinned; widening the bypass to `create` is killed (`error = <nil>` at `operation capability dependency`); deleting the vocabulary gate that precedes it is killed by name |\n183|| F2 four over-determined fixtures | **closed** | 4/4 delete to `error = <nil>` |\n184|| F3 executable substitution | **closed** | both sites, one through `Registry.AdmitProbe` |\n185|| F4 expiry upper boundary | **closed** | `expires_at == now` row; `!Before`→`After` killed |\n186|| F5 Probe/Evidence closed schemas | **closed** | 10/10 |\n187|| F6 six unevidenced classes | **closed** | M38/M39/M41/M60/M79/M81 all killed |\n188|| F7 four unmeasured bounds | **closed** | M45/M89/M90/M91 all killed |\n189|| F8 dead arms and inaccurate doc | **closed** | both arms gone, doc corrected |\n190|| F9 README + traceability | **closed** — see below | |\n191|| G-A-1 claim-slice aliasing | **closed** | see G-C |\n192|\n193|---\n194|\n195|## G-C — the aliasing pin\n196|\n197|I re-ran round 1's exact positive control: `parseClaim` returning\n198|`row.dependentOperations` / `row.evidenceRequirements` instead of the parsed\n199|slices. Round 1: all 52 tests passed. Round 2:\n200|\n201|```\n202|manifest_pin_test.go:166: capabilityRegistry[\"headless_creation\"] =\n203|  {generationVariable:true dependentOperations:[REVIEW-POISON]\n204|   evidenceRequirements:[REVIEW-POISON REVIEW-POISON]} after claim mutation,\n205|  want {... dependentOperations:[create] evidenceRequirements:[conformance_fixture runtime_probe]}\n206|```\n207|\n208|`TestParsedClaimSlicesShareNoRegistryBacking` catches it, and five other tests\n209|fall over behind it. The pin re-reads the interior (row snapshot,\n210|`CapabilitiesForOperation`, a fresh admission) rather than only re-comparing\n211|`Admitted`, which is what round 1 asked for. **The claim is pinned.**\n212|\n213|Stated bound: the pin poisons only the two capabilities the fixture claims, so it\n214|directly holds 2 of the 16 registry rows. The aliasing it guards is structural in\n215|`parseClaim`'s single return statement, so one path covers all rows — a bound on\n216|the fixture, not a gap in the guard.\n217|\n218|---\n219|\n220|## F9 — verified by attacking the gate, not by reading the diff\n221|\n222|`terminal-manifest-probe-admission` is registered against production\n223|`internal/terminalbackend/manifest.go` `AdmitProbe` with 11 tests; the projection\n224|digest is re-pinned to `892bdb6a…bf806a035`; README and the golden figure move\n225|74→75. The gate has teeth — all five attacks killed:\n226|\n227|| Attack | Result |\n228|| --- | --- |\n229|| acceptance case names a test that does not exist | KILLED — `declaration \"TestNoSuchReviewTest\" is absent from …` |\n230|| acceptance case names a production declaration that does not exist | KILLED — same shape, caught by the README figures pin too |\n231|| `gap` prose edited without re-pinning the digest | KILLED — projection digest differs from reviewed |\n232|| the whole new acceptance case removed | KILLED — projection digest differs |\n233|| reviewed digest reverted to leaf 1's value | KILLED |\n234|\n235|The README `15.3#3` sentence is now true: the clause forbids Structured Error\n236|from appearing as an RPC hello key or a TerminalBackend capability, and the\n237|re-scoped text claims only that the admission registry is not on a hello path\n238|and carries no Structured Error code, with the clause left **undischarged**.\n239|I checked the substantive half independently — the 16 capability names are\n240|disjoint from `internal/axerror`'s code registry — so the prose is accurate.\n241|\n242|Two observations, neither blocking:\n243|\n244|* That disjointness is asserted in prose in the ownership `gap` field and held by\n245|  no test. It supports a *non*-discharge, so it is not an unsupported discharge\n246|  claim, but it is checkable and unchecked.\n247|* The `section:4.B`, `4.C`, `4.D`, `6.5` and `7.A` bindings still name\n248|  `internal/catalog/catalog.go` `ForRelease` with `coverage: unevidenced` and\n249|  `clauses: 0`, and `unevidenced=41` / `clauses_discharged=17/403` are unchanged\n250|  from leaf 1. Round 1 cited those numbers as context for F9; its stated\n251|  requirement was to re-scope the README sentence and register this leaf's\n252|  evidence \"as leaf 1 did\", and both are done. Leaving the section binding\n253|  unevidenced under-claims rather than over-claims, which is the correct\n254|  direction — self-minting a coverage upgrade would be the worse error. Recorded\n255|  so the next leaf can decide whether §4.B's owner should move.\n256|\n257|---\n258|\n259|## What is good, stated so the next rework does not undo it\n260|\n261|* The four narrowed F2 fixtures are the right kind of narrow: each was tightened\n262|  until only the named rule can reject it, and the deletion probe proves it by\n263|  reaching admission rather than by landing elsewhere. Do not re-widen them.\n264|* The F3 fixture's probe deliberately tracks the foreign digest. That is the only\n265|  way to separate two arms sharing both code and detail. The comment above it\n266|  explains why — keep it.\n267|* `requireRefusal`/`requirePinRefusal` taking `(code, detail)` is the structural\n268|  fix. Every new refusal case must go through them.\n269|* `TestParsedClaimSlicesShareNoRegistryBacking` is a real interior pin, not a\n270|  restatement of `TestAdmittedSharesNoBackingArrays`. Keep both.\n271|* The logbook entry names the mis-targeted expiry test, the deterministic-RSA\n272|  dedup surprise and the nil-vs-empty production bug. That honesty is the reason\n273|  this round had a real baseline to measure against.\n274|\n275|## Stated bounds — what this method cannot see\n276|\n277|* **JCS remains unprovable** with these fixtures (`M71`/`M72`). Unchanged by the\n278|  rework, and unfixable without a fixture carrying a non-ASCII key or a number,\n279|  which the closed schemas forbid. Reported as unknown.\n280|* `M92`/`M95` are near-equivalent mutants counted as survivors for honesty.\n281|* 34 of 119 arm sites are never executed. Beyond H1-H3 the remainder are\n282|  `document identity` (2), `evidence canonical bytes` (2) — `json.Marshal`/\n283|  `jcs.Transform` failures on already-validated maps, practically unreachable —\n284|  `registry unavailable` (= `M92`), and repeated `document members`/`document\n285|  member type`/`document syntax`/`document timestamp` sites whose rule is\n286|  asserted at another site.\n287|* Arm→coverage mapping uses the narrowest containing block, so the 85/119\n288|  executed figure is an upper bound on sites reached.\n289|* Mutation bounds the suite, not the code. A killed mutant means the arm is\n290|  exercised, not that it matches the spec. Round 1's F1 was the standing proof.\n291|* Nothing measured under `-race`; concurrency is out of scope for this leaf.\n292|\n293|---\n294|\n295|## Required for acceptance\n296|\n297|1. **H1** — add the `TestReconcileRefusals` row for a static-origin probe claim\n298|   absent from the manifest, asserting `probe static claim without manifest`.\n299|   Verify by neutering that `case` arm and confirming the new row fails.\n300|2. **H2** — add an `evidence platform` case to `TestEvidenceDocumentRefusals`.\n301|3. **H3** — add a non-object claim element case asserting `claim shape`.\n302|\n303|All three are single table rows. Nothing else in this change needs to move — do\n304|not re-run the fixtures through a wider refactor. Re-run both batteries plus the\n305|17-gate arm-shift probe afterwards; the acceptance target is 91/95 held with the\n306|same four declared survivors, and 17/17 still failing with `error = <nil>`."}}}
